{"records":[{"id":"j2wjdkj5cn","title":"Flack et al., Wnt-dependent Inactivation of the Groucho/TLE Co-repressor by the HECT E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Hyd/UBR5, Molecular Cell (2017)","description":"Uncropped western blot and microscopy imaging data for Flack et al., Wnt-dependent Inactivation of the Groucho/TLE Co-repressor by the HECT E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Hyd/UBR5, Molecular Cell (2017).\n\nNote that many blots were cut at appropriate heights to avoid having to rerun gels or having to strip/reprobe blots when blotting with multiple antibodies. In some cases, only relevant parts of film were scanned.\n\nPlease cite this article as: Flack et al., Wnt-Dependent Inactivation of the Groucho/TLE Co-repressor by the HECT E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Hyd/ UBR5, Molecular Cell (2017), http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.molcel.2017.06.009","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-06-26T16:01:44.736Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j2wjdkj5cn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j2wjdkj5cn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Flack","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariann","last_name":"Bienz","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Developmental Biology","Drosophila","Fluorescence Microscopy","Gel Electrophoresis","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"32h94v2yv2","title":"Elucidation of E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Specificity . Zhang et al.Through Proteome-wide Degron Mapping. Zhang et al.","description":"This repository contains sequencing data related to \"Elucidation of E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Specificity Through Proteome-wide Degron Mapping\" by Zhang et al. Data is included for the GPS peptidome screen with and without MLN, results from the composition-based SVM machine learning model, along with sequencing data for scanning and saturation mutagenesis degron footprints, and from CRISPR screens for identification of E3-degron pairs. Westernblot raw images from cycloheximide chase and co-immunoprecipitation assays are also included.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-21T06:47:33.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/32h94v2yv2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/32h94v2yv2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhiqian","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brandon","last_name":"Sie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aiquan","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumei","last_name":"Leng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Nardone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephen ","last_name":"Elledge","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Degradation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h6rn55h86x","title":"The Human E3 ligase RNF185 is a regulator of the SARS-CoV-2 envelope protein. Zou et al","description":"Supplementary data sets related to The Human E3 ligase RNF185 is a regulator of the SARS-CoV-2 envelope protein by Zou et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-27T13:44:21.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h6rn55h86x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h6rn55h86x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mikolaj","last_name":"Slabicki","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6k6ng65623","title":"Generation of host-directed and virus-specific antivirals using targeted protein degradation promoted by small molecules and viral RNA mimics","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD), as exemplified by proteolysis-targeting chimera (PROTAC) is an emerging drug discovery platform. PROTAC molecules, which typically contain a target protein ligand linked to an E3 ligase ligand, recruit a target protein to the E3 ligase to induce its ubiquitination and degradation. Here, we applied PROTAC approaches to develop broad-spectrum antivirals targeting key host factors for many viruses and virus-specific antivirals targeting unique viral proteins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-30T20:02:36.523Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6k6ng65623","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6k6ng65623"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Ho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanye","last_name":"Meng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan ","last_name":"Marazzi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Antiviral"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65k3y4smr5","title":"ATG5 attenuates inflammatory signaling in embryonic stem cells to control differentiation","description":"Attenuated inflammatory responses to both inflammation-related cytokines and infectious agents are recognized as a unique property of embryonic stem cells (ESCs). Although an increasing number of studies have reported this attenuation, the underlying molecular mechanisms are still unclear. Moreover, whether the attenuated inflammatory status is involved in ESC differentiation is completely unknown. Here, we found that the autophagy-related protein ATG5 is essential for both the attenuated inflammatory response and the differentiation of ESCs, and that the attenuation of inflammatory signaling is required for ESC differentiation. Mechanistically, ATG5 promotes 26S proteasome-mediated degradation of the SCF (SKP1-cullin-1-F-box-protein) E3 ubiquitin ligase β-TrCP1 (beta-transducin repeat containing E3 ubiquitin protein ligase 1), which is a well-known positive regulator of NF-κB signaling and the inflammatory response. Another SCF E3 ubiquitin ligase, FBXW7 (F-box and WD repeat domain containing 7), which is recruited by ATG5, was found to be the E3 ubiquitin ligase responsible for the ubiquitination and degradation of β-TrCP1. Moreover, the differentiation defects observed in ATG5-depleted ESCs are due to the accumulation of β-TrCP1 and the related hyperactivation of NF-κB signaling, as loss of β-TrCP1 and inhibition of NF-κB signaling rescued the differentiation defects in ATG5-depleted ESCs. Therefore, this study reveals a previously uncharacterized mechanism employed by ESCs to maintain the inflammatory response in an attenuated state and further provides novel insight into the biological roles of ATG5, especially in the control of the inflammatory response and differentiation of ESCs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-22T08:57:47.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65k3y4smr5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65k3y4smr5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo-Wen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying-Cui","last_name":"Wan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bei-Bei","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian-Ru","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia-Ni","last_name":"Shang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen-Ge","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yan","last_name":"Xi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Su","last_name":"Chen","orcid":"0000-0001-8804-8989"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"ZR2021QC181"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"222300420028"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82273067"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81972650"}],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zkgmjzrcyk","title":"High definition analysis of host protein stability during human cytomegalovirus infection reveals antiviral factors and viral evasion mechanisms, Nightingale et al.","description":"Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is an important pathogen with multiple immune evasion strategies, including virally facilitated degradation of host antiviral restriction factors. Here, we describe a multiplexed approach to discover proteins with innate immune function on the basis of active degradation by the proteasome or lysosome during early phase HCMV infection. Using three orthogonal proteomic/transcriptomic screens to quantify protein degradation, with high confidence we identified 35 proteins enriched in antiviral restriction factors. A final screen employed a comprehensive panel of viral mutants to predict viral genes that target >250 human proteins. This approach revealed Helicase-like Transcription Factor (HLTF), a DNA helicase important in DNA repair, potently inhibits early viral gene expression but is rapidly degraded during infection. The functionally unknown HCMV protein UL145 facilitates HLTF degradation by recruiting the Cullin4 E3 ligase complex. Our approach and data will enable further identifications of innate pathways targeted by HCMV and other viruses.\n\nSee https://www.cell.com/cell-host-microbe/fulltext/S1931-3128(18)30381-0    for full publication.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-19T19:48:42.342Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zkgmjzrcyk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zkgmjzrcyk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Virology","Proteomics","Human Cytomegalovirus"],"journal_issn":["19313128"]},{"id":"s7thkp9z4r","title":"The CBL-LSD1-CXCL8 Axis Regulates Methionine Metabolism in Glioma","description":"We found that CXCL8 expression was significantly increased in a methionine restricted environment due to the upregulation of H3K4 methylation. Mechanistically, the E3 ubiquitin ligase CBL mediated the ubiquitinated degradation of histone demethylase LSD1, reducing LSD1 protein stability and enhancing H3K4me1 modification of the CXCL8 gene.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-24T09:21:30.923Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s7thkp9z4r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s7thkp9z4r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"jie","last_name":"chang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolism","Glioma","Methionine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3zygrwcj4t","title":"The contribution of native protein complexes to targeted protein degradation","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD) destroys proteins of interest (POIs) by hijacking the cellular proteolytic machinery. Most proteins in cells exist and function as part of multi-protein or macromolecular complexes, thereby allowing a single protein to control multiple biological processes. Therefore, when a small molecule degrader induces proximity between an E3 ligase and the POI, the macromolecular context of the POI potentially influences the degradation outcomes of the POI and of the complex components. Here, we explore degradation of the eight CK1α-SACK1 (formerly known as FAM83A-H) complexes initiated by molecular glue degraders primarily designed to target Ser/Thr kinase CK1α. We demonstrate that lenalidomide-derived degraders DEG-77 and SJ3149, which selectively target the CK1α isoform, co-degrade multiple SACK1 proteins. We show that the degradation of SACK1 proteins by DEG-77 and SJ3149 requires CK1α, the CUL4ACRBN E3 ligase complex and the proteasome. In cells derived from palmoplantar keratoderma patients harbouring the CK1α-binding deficient SACK1GR265P mutation, DEG-77 targets CK1α and mitotic SACK1D but not SACK1GR265P, highlighting the requirement for CK1α-SACK1 interaction to achieve co-degradation. Our study underscores the importance of POI context in TPD and reinforces the potential for selectively targeting specific protein complexes for degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-29T13:52:59.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3zygrwcj4t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3zygrwcj4t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorraine","last_name":"Glennie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicole","last_name":"Curnutt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gajanan","last_name":"Sathe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brune","last_name":"Le Chatelier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Freya ","last_name":"Goff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin-Feng","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyrell ","last_name":"Cartwright","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karen","last_name":"Dunbar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicola","last_name":"Wood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Macartney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christina ","last_name":"Woo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gopal","last_name":"Sapkota","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Protein Degradation","Chemical Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139152,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":388,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139152,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138534,"restricted":66,"embargoed":552},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2325},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1432},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":659},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":476},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":307},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":273}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136503},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":136},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":921},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":875},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":523},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":363}]}}